\paragraph{A layer-normalised target in detail (A4).}
The model is the linear JEPA with $n=8$ input directions, $d\in\{10,20\}$, $P_0=p_0I$, weight decay $\lambda$ on $W$ and $P$, gradient descent, and the target $\mathrm{LN}(y)=\Pi y/\sqrt{|\Pi y|^2/d+\epsilon}$ ($\Pi$ removes the mean, $\epsilon=10^{-5}$, no affine, as I-JEPA's \texttt{F.layer\_norm}) under stop-gradient.
Because the context is linear-Gaussian given the target view, Stein's lemma gives exactly $\mathbb E[\mathrm{LN}(Wx_t)x_c^\top]=G(W)W\Sigma_{tc}$ with $G(W)=\mathbb E\,J_\mathrm{LN}(Wx_t)$, so the gradients are the plain model's with $W\Sigma_{tc}$ replaced by $GW\Sigma_{tc}$ (N2-i).
For $\epsilon\to0$ the drive $GW$ has degree zero in $W$; a single remaining direction is driven at a rate independent of its amplitude, so the origin is a saddle and the rank stays $\ge1$ for starts outside a basin of size set by $\epsilon$, which every grid point is (N2-ii).
A small direction among $k\ge2$ active ones still races, with $c_k$ replaced by $\Gamma_0c_k$ (N2-ii); a fixed point for direction $i$ exists iff $\Gamma_ic_i\ge2\sqrt{\lambda\sigma_i}$ with $\Gamma_i$ set by the whole active set, so existence is necessary, but several self-consistent sets can exist and the race selects among them (N2-iv); iterating existence alone from the all-alive start gives the baseline \texttt{ln\_static}.
For an orthogonal initialisation the per-direction ansatz is preserved exactly and gives a flow coupled only through the gains $\Gamma_i$ (N2-iii, Monte Carlo over 2048 fixed draws); N2 applies it to the registered Gaussian initialisation, where it is an approximation.
Derivations and checks are in \texttt{theory/ext\_A\_lntarget.md} and \texttt{results/e4/a4\_theory\_checks.json}.
The registration (\texttt{results/e4/prediction\_a4.json}, revision 3, before any held-out truth) fixed the criterion, the must-beat rows, the readings of each outcome and a floor: every run is re-run on a fresh sample of the target view, a run is fragile if its rank changes at all, and above 5\% fragile the word comes from the sample-robust runs.
On \ExtAfourRuns{} held-out runs (two spectrum families, $d$, $p_0$, $\lambda$, $w_0$ and seeds disjoint from development) \ExtAfourFragile{} runs (\ExtAfourFragShare{}) are fragile, every one by exactly one, so the word is ``pass on sample-robust runs''; the all-run word is the same.
The flips belong to the truth's finite cross-term sample (4096 draws), whereas N2 predicts the population dynamics.
On the \ExtAfourRobust{} robust runs N2 errs by \ExtAfourMae{} with every run within one (all runs \ExtAfourAllMae{}), against \ExtAfourStaticMae{} for the static bound, \ExtAfourRaceMae{} for the delivered race, \ExtAfourMuMae{} for the $\mu$ count, \ExtAfourMuLnMae{} with the gain at the all-alive state inserted into it (our adaptation: \citet{demelocosta2026drive} do not model a target LayerNorm), \ExtAfourLnMae{} for the LN fixed-point set from the all-alive start and \ExtAfourEarlyMae{} for the count at $0.1T$ (MAEs on all runs).
The differences to N2 (\cref{tab:ext-tests}) are \ExtAfourStaticDiff{} ($\ExtAfourStaticCI$), \ExtAfourRaceDiff{} ($\ExtAfourRaceCI$), \ExtAfourMuDiff{} ($\ExtAfourMuCI$; every $\mu$ variant is beaten), \ExtAfourMuLnDiff{} ($\ExtAfourMuLnCI$) and \ExtAfourLnDiff{} ($\ExtAfourLnCI$; \ExtAfourLnDiffD{}, $\ExtAfourLnCID$, on the \ExtAfourLnRunsD{} runs where the two counts differ); N2 also beats the all-alive count (\ExtAfourAliveDiff{}) and the development median (\ExtAfourDevMedDiff{}), so it predicts more than a constant on this grid, and it is at least as good as the count at $0.1T$ (\ExtAfourEarlyDiff{}, $\ExtAfourEarlyCI$), not better than measurement.
The registered signs hold: rank does not rise with $\lambda$ (\ExtAfourLamDec{} decreases, no increase, in \ExtAfourLamPairs{} pairs) nor fall with $d$ (\ExtAfourDInc{} increases, no decrease, in \ExtAfourDPairs{}), $w_0$ acts weakly, and every run keeps a direction; the sign of $p_0$ was not registered and goes both ways (\ExtAfourPzeroInc{} increases, \ExtAfourPzeroDec{} decreases in \ExtAfourPzeroPairs{} pairs).
With an orthogonal initialisation N2 errs by \ExtAfourOrthMae{} on 96 configurations, against \ExtAfourGaussSameMae{} for the Gaussian one (\ExtAfourGaussTwenty{} at $d=20$, where most fragile runs lie).
The scope is gradient descent; AdamW with a normalised target is not covered.
By the exit fixed before A4's outcome, the ladder's rung 4 puts this target on rung 2's MLP encoder (below).

\paragraph{Weight-decay schedules in detail.}\label{sec:sched}
With $\lambda_P(t)=\rho\lambda_W(t)$ the race runs on the clock $\int_0^t\lambda_P$; a stage model adds that a captured direction is lost, after a delay, once $\lambda_W(t)$ passes $c_k^2/(4\sigma_k)$, and that the predictor then decays at the slower of $\alpha\lambda_P$ and its drive's rate.
It predicts that an increasing schedule ends at most at the static count of its final value, and that a warm-up switched before the low-decay race completes can end \emph{below} the constant high-decay count, because low decay slows the predictor's clock.
On the \ExtBRuns{} held-out runs the frozen model follows the alive state after the first schedule change on \ExtBAgree{} of the pairs where it or the truth changes (bar $0.90$) and the final count with error \ExtBFinalMae{} (final-value static count \ExtBFinalStaticNow{}, $\mu$ count \ExtBMuFinal{}).
As registered it fails: \ExtBCellsFail{} of \ExtBCells{} cells, all short high pulses with $\alpha\rho\le1$, fall below the cell bar $0.80$, and the predictor after a loss is missed by a median $|\log|$ ratio of \ExtBPostLoss{} (bar $0.5$).
The warm-up sign and the increasing-schedule bound hold; early and late decreases fail (below).
The held-out grid has steps, ramps and pulses with $\alpha\in\{0.5,1,2\}$, $\rho\in\{0.25,\dots,2\}$ and an EMA target.
For $\alpha=\rho=1$ and a copy target the balance \eqref{eq:balance} holds with $\lambda t$ replaced by $\int_0^t\lambda$, so the direction is an exact non-autonomous scalar system.
After a loss $p/w$ rises, is held or falls as $\lambda_W-\alpha\lambda_P$ is positive, zero or negative; the slaved relation $p=w\sqrt{\lambda_W/\lambda_P}$ of the development stage model holds after a loss only for $\alpha\lambda_P=\lambda_W$, and the frozen model replaces it by the larger of the predictor's own decay and its static nullcline.
On the held-out grid the development stage model agrees on \ExtBAgreeVone{} of the changed pairs and capture followed by the static threshold on \ExtBAgreeCapStatic{} (the frozen model's margin in agreement over all pairs where any predictor changes state: CI $\ExtBVoneCI$ and $\ExtBCapStaticCI$) and the static threshold at the current value on \ExtBAgreeStaticNow{}; post-change events have \ExtBUnmatched{} unmatched against \ExtBUnmatchedCapStatic{} for capture-static (90th percentile timing \ExtBTimePninety{}), and the final count errs by \ExtBFinalCapStatic{} under capture-static.
The agreement above is over \ExtBPairs{} changed pairs, and post-change events are timed to a median $|\log|$ ratio of \ExtBTimeMed{}; the warm-up sign holds in \ExtBWarmSign{} of \ExtBWarmPairs{} pairs and the bound for increasing schedules has \ExtBIncViol{} violations in \ExtBIncRuns{} runs.
A late decrease revives what the model predicts in \ExtBLateAgree{} of runs (bar $0.90$), and in \ExtBLateZero{} of late-decrease runs nothing is revived; an early decrease is within one of the lower constant in \ExtBEarlyWithin{} of pairs, and in the frozen model itself in \ExtBEarlyModel{}, so that consequence was wrong rather than missed.
The $\mu$ count agrees on \ExtBMuAgree{} of the changed pairs, and as a bound on the final count it is exceeded in \ExtBMuExceed{} of runs.
The per-cell bar applies to the \ExtBCells{} of the \ExtBCellsAll{} (kind $\times$ variant) cells with at least 100 changed pairs; none of the three failing cells (\ExtBShort{}) is flagged ill-posed.
Over the grid the truth regains \ExtBRegainTruth{} directions after a loss and the frozen model \ExtBRegainModel{}, \ExtBRegainShared{} of them the same (reported, not scored).
Read post hoc, the short-pulse failure lies in the predictor after a loss, which decides whether a direction lost during the pulse regrows: the sign of the truth's deviation from the slaved relation follows $\mathrm{sign}(\lambda_W-\alpha\lambda_P)$ in every held-out cell, as stated before the score was read, but this is not a scored statistic; the law registered from it fails on held-out runs (B1$'$, below).

\paragraph{Post-loss law (B1$'$).}
B1$'$ keeps E5's frozen stage model until the model itself loses a direction and from then on, while the direction stays dead, moves its predictor entry by the linearised law $\dot p=-\alpha\lambda_Pp+\alpha c\,w^2$ (copy target).
It was registered with E5's truth, floors and tolerances; an amendment written before any record was read makes the prospective test a new grid (\ExtBpRuns{} runs) whose statistics, plateau values, $p_0$ and $\alpha\rho$ values no development step used, and the grid built like the development pilot (\ExtBpRepRuns{} runs) a seed replication; no run was non-finite (rows in \cref{tab:ext-tests}).
It fails as registered on both.
B1a$'$: agreement \ExtBpAgree{} overall, but \ExtBpCellsFail{} short-pulse cells fall below $0.80$ (\ExtBpRepCellsFail{} in the replication), and as differences in disagreement (baseline $-$ model) E5's model agrees slightly more often ($\ExtBpDisQs$), the earlier stage model is not shown worse ($\ExtBpDisVone$) and only the $\mu$ count is ($\ExtBpDisMu$).
B1b$'$: median $|\log|$ timing ratio \ExtBpTimeMed{} with \ExtBpUnm{} of events unmatched (bars $0.10$ and 5\%; replication \ExtBpRepUnm{} unmatched).
B1d: median $|\log|$ post-loss error \ExtBpD{} (\ExtBpRepD{}; bar $0.5$), failing in every $\alpha\rho$ class ($<1$, $=1$, $1$--$2$, $>2$: \ExtBpDClass{}); the replication passes only at $\alpha\rho<1$ (\ExtBpRepDLow{}), which does not carry the verdict.
What the law adds is its own claim, which passes on both grids: against E5's model its post-loss error falls from \ExtBpQs{} to \ExtBpD{} (difference $\ExtBpQsCI$) and from \ExtBpRepQs{} to \ExtBpRepD{} ($\ExtBpRepQsCI$), about a third.
The remaining error has one sign (median signed $\log(|p_{\mathrm{truth}}|/p_{\mathrm{law}})$ \ExtBpSigned{} against median absolute \ExtBpD{}; replication \ExtBpRepSigned{}), so the law places the dead direction's predictor entry below the true one; the score files do not measure how much of it is present at the first dead check.
The registered rate check passes pooled (\ExtBpRate{}; E5's model \ExtBpRateQs{}, no-decay null \ExtBpRateNull{}) but fails at $\alpha\rho$ between 1 and 2 (\ExtBpRateMid{}; replication \ExtBpRepRateMid{}), as E5's model does, and the rate advantage over E5's model is resolved on the prospective grid only ($\ExtBpRateQsCI$ against $\ExtBpRepRateQsCI$).
The failure has the same place on both grids, short pulses and $\alpha\rho$ between 1 and 2, and B1's verdict stands.

\paragraph{Minibatch noise (B3).}
B3 trains the aligned linear JEPA with $n=16$ modes and a copy target by minibatch SGD on fresh Gaussian samples, at batch sizes $B\in\{2,8,32\}$ and with population gradients, over two values each of $\lambda$, $p_0$ and $\eta$ (\ExtBthRuns{} held-out runs, \ExtBthDiverged{} diverged; registration and an amendment of its scoring both before any held-out run; rows in \cref{tab:ext-tests}).
The noise is multiplicative and every term acting on a dead output direction is proportional to it, so in this model reseeding and an additive floor are excluded by construction; S1 (no re-escape: \ExtBthReesc{} of \ExtBthNoisy{} noisy runs) is therefore a consistency check, not evidence against a floor, and the noise clause does not cover a lagging EMA target, additive noise or nonlinear layers.
S2, that noise drives dead directions lower than the population run (median $\log_{10}$ of the largest dead singular value relative to the top: $B=2<B=32<$ population at each $\eta$), fails: at $\eta=0.02$, $B=32$ ends at $\ExtBthSTwoBtt$ against $\ExtBthSTwoPop$.
Split post hoc, the order holds in all \ExtBthSTwoLoHold{} $\lambda\times\eta$ cells at $p_0=0.7$ and fails in all \ExtBthSTwoHiFail{} at $p_0=1.5$, where $W$ first collapses as a whole and leaves the population run's dead directions deepest, at the same levels in float64 and 80-bit state.
S3 is undetermined: noise moves the count by \ExtBthSThree{} directions on average against the population run, inside the bar of $1.5$, but by \ExtBthSThreeSens{} when the diverged run counts as a miss; the shift account of prior work changes no count here (it equals the population race on every noisy run), the count read at $0.1T$ does as well (\ExtBthEarly; difference $\ExtBthEarlyCI$, not resolved), and the numerical exponents (\ExtBthMc), the window and every $\mu$ variant (\ExtBthWindow) and the static bound (\ExtBthStatic) do worse.
S4 passes: the sign of the noise effect follows $p_0$ ($\ExtBthSFourLo$ at $p_0=0.7$, $\ExtBthSFourHi$ at $p_0=1.5$; by $\lambda=0.006, 0.012$: $\ExtBthDLoA$, $\ExtBthDLoB$ and $\ExtBthDHiA$, $\ExtBthDHiB$), replicating the development pattern on held-out runs and unchanged in 80-bit state; no account here predicts its sign or size.
At $p_0=1.5$, \ExtBthRegrow{} directions of noisy runs that the initial collapse takes below $10^{-13}$ of the top singular value grow once their window opens, where registration expected none; they are the same in 80-bit state (\ExtBthRegrowF), where the \ExtBthDepthDeeper{} that bottom at float64's floor are resolved and \ExtBthDepthSameCross{} of \ExtBthDepthEvents{} cross the threshold at the same check, so they are the exact dynamics and not re-escapes, while float32 state, whose resolution lies above those levels, changes \ExtBthFthirtytwo{} of \ExtBthFthirtytwoRuns{} final counts at $p_0=1.5$.
These are statements about this linear model; whether noise that breaks the invariance creates a floor is untested.

\paragraph{Deep linear encoder.}
With identity predictor $p_0I$, copy target and factor amplitudes $w_1,\dots,w_L$ of direction $k$, $\dot w_i=\gamma(p)a^2/w_i-\lambda w_i$ and $\dot p=(c-p\sigma)a^2-\lambda p$, with $a=\prod_iw_i$.
Then $\frac{d}{dt}(w_i^2-w_j^2)=-2\lambda(w_i^2-w_j^2)$ and $\frac{d}{dt}(p^2-w_i^2)=-2\lambda(p^2-w_i^2)$, which give the reduction of \cref{sec:deep}.
With $p_r=p_0e^{-\lambda t}$ and $u=a^m$, $\dot u=mL\gamma u^2-mL\lambda u$, so $v=1/u$ solves a linear equation and $v(t)=e^{(2L-2)\lambda t}\big[a_0^{-m}-(2L-2)I(t)\big]$ with $I(t)=\int_0^t\gamma(p_r)e^{-(2L-2)\lambda\tau}d\tau$; $u$ blows up in finite time, i.e.\ the direction escapes, if and only if \eqref{eq:deep} holds.
A1 drops $a^{2/L}$ beside $p_0^2$ in $p$, which is not small for $L=3$ near threshold.
On the \ExtCRuns{} held-out runs A1 errs by \ExtCAlMae{} ($\ExtCAlMaeCI$) with aligned factors, against \ExtCAlRaceOne{} for the depth-one race, \ExtCAlStaticL{} for the depth-$L$ static bound, \ExtCAlWindow{} for the initial window (the end-to-end $\mu$ count) and \ExtCAlEarlyFifty{} for the count read at $t=50$; with generic factors it errs by \ExtCGeMae{} against \ExtCGeEarlyFifty{} for that reading (difference CI $\ExtCGeEarlyFiftyCI$), which fails the registered test. At $0.1T$ the measured count errs by \ExtCAlTrajTen{} (aligned) and \ExtCGeTrajTen{} (generic) (\cref{fig:ext}b).
The $\mu$ count of \citet{demelocosta2026drive}, applied to the end-to-end map, equals the initial window on all \ExtCRuns{} runs (error \ExtCMuAl{} aligned and \ExtCMuGe{} generic for $L\ge2$; added after the score, from inputs only); linearised literally at $W_1=\dots=W_L=0$ it is degenerate for $L\ge2$, since every gradient term in $W_i$ carries the other factors.
\ExtCAlZero{} of aligned and \ExtCGeZero{} of generic runs end at rank zero. On the informative runs (\ExtCAlInfRuns{} aligned, \ExtCGeInfRuns{} generic; post hoc) A1 errs by \ExtCAlInfMae{} and \ExtCGeInfMae{}, the reading at $t=50$ by \ExtCAlInfEarly{} and \ExtCGeInfEarly{}.

\paragraph{Deep predictor with random factors (rung 1b).}
With a depth-one encoder and a predictor $P=Q_M\cdots Q_1$ whose factors are arbitrary, weight decay $\lambda$ on every factor and a copy target, the predictor's gradient near capture is the rank-one $-(c-p_u\sigma)a^2uu^\top$ along the latent direction $u$ of the growing mode, so $p_u=u^\top Pu$ obeys $\dot p_u=\alpha[(c-p_u\sigma)a^2K_u-M\lambda p_u]$ with $K_u=\sum_{j=1}^M|Q_{>j}^\top u|^2|Q_{<j}u|^2$ ($Q_{>j}=Q_M\cdots Q_{j+1}$, $Q_{<j}=Q_{j-1}\cdots Q_1$), and the mode captures the predictor once $a^2(c-p_u\sigma)K_u\ge M\lambda p_u$.
For identity factors $K_u=Mp^{2-2/M}$ and this is the E1c correction (\cref{app:more}); for $M=1$ it is the depth-one rule.
Before capture each factor only decays, so $K_u(t)=e^{-2(M-1)\alpha\lambda t}K_u(0)$; the rule is applied to each singular triplet of the integrated linear system with the actual $W_0$ and $P_0$.
It was registered after development on separate statistics and scored once on \ExtCbRuns{} held-out runs ($M=1$--$4$, Gaussian or perturbed-identity factors; $M=1$ controls), with two verdict cells pooling $M=2$--$4$.
Both pass, although with Gaussian factors the count read at $0.1T$ does better (below): with Gaussian factors error \ExtCbGaMae{} ($\ExtCbGaMaeCI$), within one in \ExtCbGaWithin{}; with perturbed-identity factors \ExtCbNiMae{} ($\ExtCbNiMaeCI$), within one in \ExtCbNiWithin{}.
Every registered baseline is worse with the paired CI above zero: the depth-one rule \ExtCbGaDone{} and \ExtCbNiDone{} (CIs $\ExtCbGaDoneCI$, $\ExtCbNiDoneCI$), the identity-factor race \ExtCbGaRaceId{} and \ExtCbNiRaceId{}, the static bound \ExtCbGaStatic{} and \ExtCbNiStatic{}, and the $\mu$ count of \citet{demelocosta2026drive} in its best variant (paired on the predictor) \ExtCbGaMu{} and \ExtCbNiMu{} (main-text form \ExtCbGaMuMain{} and \ExtCbNiMuMain{}).
Reported beside the verdict: the count read at $0.1T$ beats the rule with Gaussian factors (\ExtCbGaEarlyTen{}, difference CI $\ExtCbGaEarlyTenCI$) and loses with perturbed-identity factors (\ExtCbNiEarlyTen{}, CI $\ExtCbNiEarlyTenCI$); with Gaussian factors the gain over the depth-one rule is not resolved at a single depth ($M=2$: $\ExtCbGaMtwoDoneCI$; $M=3$: $\ExtCbGaMthreeDoneCI$), and at $M=4$ the rule does not beat the count read at $t=50$ ($\ExtCbGaMfourEarlyCI$).
\ExtCbFragile{} of the \ExtCbRuns{} runs are fragile under the truth floor; one single-depth cell (Gaussian, $M=2$) exceeds the registered 5\% and is flagged ill-posed, while both verdict cells are below it.
The frozen scorer was corrected once before any score was read: in runs with $T=500$ the early time $t=50$ coincides with $0.1T$ and was stored once; the fix reads that same-step count, and no run was repeated.

\paragraph{Two-layer MLP encoder under gradient descent (rung 2).}
With $f(x)=W_2\phi(W_1x)$ ($\phi=\tanh$ or ReLU, no biases), predictor $p_0I$, copy target and weight decay on $W_1$, $W_2$ and $P$, the gradient on $W_1$ near collapse is of the order of $|P||W_2|^2$ (the error signal $P^\top(Pf(\tilde x)-\mathrm{sg}\,f(x))$ is itself linear in $W_2$), so $W_1$ only decays, $W_1(t)=(1-\eta\lambda)^tW_1(0)$, as the predictor does before capture.
The race on the hidden features then has to use the statistics of $\phi(W_1(t)x)$, not those at initialisation; for ReLU they shrink exactly by $(1-\eta\lambda)^{2t}$, for tanh we recompute them along the decay.
This rule ($r_\text{decay}$) and the frozen-feature rule of E2's MLP arm ($r_\text{init}$) integrate the near-collapse linear system from the actual $W_2(0)$.
The derivation and its check are in \texttt{theory/ext\_C\_ladder.md} (``Rung 2'') and \texttt{results/e6/r2\_heldout/posthoc/w1path.json} (post hoc, on the held-out runs below, which it reproduces): the decay path is exact up to a correction of order $|P||W_2|^2$ before capture (gradient ratio at most \ExtRtwoGRatioMax{}); it is validated numerically up to the first escape of a direction (relative departure at most $\ExtRtwoDeltaPre$); after it $W_1$ learns, and the failure is located there: at the step where the race's count is decided the statistic error has median \ExtRtwoStatErrDec{} (\ExtRtwoStatErrLt{} in the latent tanh cell), the \ExtRtwoDecEarlyRuns{} runs that decide before the first escape have error \ExtRtwoDecEarlyMae{} and the \ExtRtwoDecLateRuns{} that decide after it \ExtRtwoDecLateMae{} (an association, not a registered test).
Development on separate grids expected $r_\text{decay}$ not to separate from the static bound or the input-statistics race $r_\text{lin}$.
The registration (before any held-out run, two amendments: independent draws per configuration, each verdict also on each half of the grid, and non-finite runs counted as misses) asked for within one $\ge0.70$, error $\le1.5$, and a configuration-cluster CI above zero against $r_\text{init}$, the initial window and every $\mu$ variant of \citet{demelocosta2026drive}.
On \ExtRtwoRuns{} held-out base runs (full-batch GD, two synthetic data models under Bernoulli masks, true ranks 0--5, mean \ExtRtwoTruthMean{}; \ExtRtwoNonfin{} non-finite) it passes: error \ExtRtwoMae{} over the \ExtRtwoFinRuns{} finite runs, within one \ExtRtwoWithin{} over all \ExtRtwoRuns{} with the non-finite runs counted as misses (\cref{tab:ext-tests}).
The frozen-feature failure is located: $r_\text{init}$ errs by \ExtRtwoInit{} (difference \ExtRtwoInitDiff{}, CI $\ExtRtwoInitCI$; halves $\ExtRtwoInitCIa$, $\ExtRtwoInitCIb$), so on this grid counting the first layer's own decay restores the race's accuracy.
Every $\mu$ variant errs by \ExtRtwoMuMin{}--\ExtRtwoMuMax{} more (differences; smallest for their exact form, CI $\ExtRtwoMuExactCI$; the initial window \ExtRtwoWinDiff{}, $\ExtRtwoWinCI$).
Comparisons fixed before the run but not part of the registered claim: on the runs where the two predictions differ by at least two from inputs alone, the race is better than the static bound (\ExtRtwoStInfRace{} against \ExtRtwoStInf{} on \ExtRtwoStInfRuns{} runs, difference \ExtRtwoStInfDiff{}, CI $\ExtRtwoStInfCI$; all runs \ExtRtwoStPoolDiff{}, $\ExtRtwoStPoolCI$) and than $r_\text{lin}$ (\ExtRtwoLinInfRace{} against \ExtRtwoLinInf{} on \ExtRtwoLinInfRuns{} runs, \ExtRtwoLinInfDiff{}, $\ExtRtwoLinInfCI$; all runs \ExtRtwoLinPoolDiff{}, $\ExtRtwoLinPoolCI$), against the development expectation (whose grid was not built to contain such runs), and from $t=0$ it predicts the final rank slightly better than a count measured at $0.1T$ (\ExtRtwoEarly{}, by \ExtRtwoEarlyDiff{}, CI $\ExtRtwoEarlyCI$), not better than measurement; every word holds on both halves and on the settled and not-fragile subsets.
In the latent tanh cell the rank still rises after $0.1T$ (mean \ExtRtwoRiseLt{}), and the race predicts that rise; E2's one-layer tanh encoder under patch masks, whose late gain the race misses, is not in this grid.
E2's delivered arm, trained by minibatch SGD on other data, is not in the grid; we scored the same rule on its \EtwoRdRuns{} runs from inputs alone, retrospectively and post hoc (its truths were known and $r_\text{decay}$ was developed after E2; \texttt{results/e2/mlp\_rdecay/}).
It errs by \EtwoRdMae{} (within one \EtwoRdWithin{}), against \EtwoMlpMae{} for the delivered frozen-feature prediction (difference \EtwoRdInitDiff{}, configuration $\times$ seed bootstrap CI $\EtwoRdInitCI$) and \EtwoRdSigned{} for the signed $\mu$ form (\EtwoRdSigDiff{}, $\EtwoRdSigCI$), so the delivered miss is accounted for retrospectively, not tested.
Its limits: the Bernoulli family alone has within one \EtwoRdBernWithin{} (error \EtwoRdBernMae{}), below rung 2's bar; at $\lambda=0.001$ the exact linearised $\mu$ form has the lower error, not separated (\EtwoRdLowLamExact{} against \EtwoRdLowLamRd{}; its error minus $r_\text{decay}$'s $\EtwoRdLowLamExactDiff$, configuration $\times$ seed CI $\EtwoRdLowLamExactCI$); the arm has three initialisations; and the SGD noise in $W_1$'s drift, the masks' sampling noise and $W_1$'s learning after $W_2$ grows are not modelled.

\paragraph{The same encoder under AdamW (rung 3).}
Rung 2's model trained by AdamW ($\eta=0.002$) has no derived count: the law of \cref{sec:adamw} needs the aligned basis and $r_\text{decay}$ needs gradient descent's decay path.
The registration (before any held-out run, two amendments) therefore takes the static bound as its criterion, with error $\le\ExtRthreeTolMae$ and within one $\ge\ExtRthreeTolWithin$, our phase model v4 as an extra row, and fixes the reading of a failure as ``no validated AdamW criterion in the MLP encoder'', not a failure of the delivered theory to transfer; development expected a failure.
The truth is the rank at the registered horizon of \ExtRthreeHorizon{} decay e-folds (median over the last 4 of 20 checks).
On \ExtRthreeRuns{} held-out base runs it fails as registered: error \ExtRthreeMae{}, within one \ExtRthreeWithin{} (bias $\ExtRthreeBias$: AdamW keeps far more directions than the bound allows), with the same word on the \ExtRthreeSetRuns{} settled runs (\ExtRthreeSetMae{}, \ExtRthreeSetWithin{}) and the \ExtRthreeNfRuns{} not-fragile ones (\ExtRthreeNfMae{}, \ExtRthreeNfWithin{}; \cref{tab:ext-tests}).
The truth is ill-posed: \ExtRthreeFragile{} of \ExtRthreeRuns{} runs change rank by more than one when the rows of $W_2(0)$ are scaled by $1+U(-10^{-3},10^{-3})$, above the registered 5\% in every cell (field tanh, field ReLU, latent tanh, latent ReLU: \ExtRthreeFragCells{}), against \ExtRtwoFragile{} of \ExtRtwoFloorRuns{} under gradient descent, and at this horizon \ExtRthreeCycling{} runs still cycle and \ExtRthreeDrifting{} drift.
It is therefore the rank at this horizon, not a final rank, and in this regime no inputs-only count can be scored to rung 2's precision.
No row comes within the tolerance.
Every $\mu$ variant errs more than the static bound or is not separated from it (differences $\ExtRthreeMuMin$ to \ExtRthreeMuMax{}; the initial window \ExtRthreeWinDiff{}, $\ExtRthreeWinCI$); only on the settled runs does the variant without weight decay do better ($\ExtRthreeNowdSetCI$).
v4 errs by \ExtRthreeVfour{} (difference CI $\ExtRthreeVfourCI$), and the best row is a measurement, the count at $0.1T$ (\ExtRthreeEarly{}, CI $\ExtRthreeEarlyCI$; \ExtRthreeEarlySet{} on the settled runs, so its word is undetermined).
The registered comparison of $r_\text{lin}$ with the static bound is not separated on the \ExtRthreeLinInfRuns{} informative runs (\ExtRthreeLinInf{} against \ExtRthreeLinInfStatic{}, CI $\ExtRthreeLinInfCI$).
Our AdamW results (\cref{sec:adamw}) therefore hold for the linear model only.

\paragraph{A layer-normalised target in the same encoder (rung 4).}
Rung 4 trains rung 2's encoder by gradient descent against the target of A4 and, by the exit fixed before A4's outcome, takes as its criterion N2 on the decaying first layer's statistics, with rung 2's tolerance (error $\le1.5$, within one $\ge0.70$).
The registration (before any held-out run, one amendment to the scorer) adds A4's rule for the word, rung 3's floor ($W_2(0)$'s rows scaled by $1+U(-10^{-3},10^{-3})$), three statements on how the criterion fails, and a conditional row that feeds N2 the run's measured first layer; development expected a failure (error \ExtRfourDevDecayMae{} on \ExtRfourDevRuns{} finite runs, about as much given the measured first layer, \ExtRfourDiagMeasMae{}, or a frozen one, \ExtRfourDiagInitMae{}; post hoc).
On \ExtRfourRuns{} held-out base runs (field and latent data, tanh and ReLU, none diverged) the truth is ill-posed: \ExtRfourFragShare{} of runs change rank under the floor (\ExtRfourFragGtOne{} by more than one, nearly all of them tanh), too few are sample-robust, and the registered word is ``undetermined (truth ill-posed)''.
Each subset taken alone is outside the tolerance: the criterion errs by \ExtRfourMae{} on all runs (within one \ExtRfourWithin{}, bias $\ExtRfourBias$), \ExtRfourRobMae{} (\ExtRfourRobWithin{}) on the \ExtRfourRobRuns{} sample-robust ones and \ExtRfourSetMae{} (\ExtRfourSetWithin{}) on the \ExtRfourSetRuns{} settled ones, and no row came within rung 2's tolerance on any subset of this grid (\cref{tab:ext-tests}).
So there is no validated LN criterion in the MLP encoder.
The registered statements hold on held-out runs.
The premise fails: under an LN target the first layer leaves its decay path within $T/20$ on every run (relative departure at least \ExtRfourDepMin{}, median \ExtRfourDepMed{}, against rung 2's \ExtRfourDepRtwo{} up to the first escape), which an order-of-magnitude argument, not a theorem, explains: in both of the target's phases (gain about $1/\sqrt\epsilon$ while $|\Pi z|^2/d\ll\epsilon$, unit scale after) the drive on $W_1$ far exceeds rung 2's at the same $|W_2|$ (\texttt{theory/ext\_C\_ladder.md}, ``Rung 4'').
How the criterion fails: it under-counts on all \ExtRfourTanhRuns{} tanh runs (bias $\ExtRfourTanhBias$, $\ExtRfourTanhBiasCI$), and the tanh rank still grows after the count read at $0.1T$ (by more than one on \ExtRfourRise{}, $\ExtRfourRiseCI$; on the only \ExtRfourRobTanhRuns{} sample-robust tanh runs \ExtRfourRobRise{}, $\ExtRfourRobRiseCI$); neither is a cause.
The conditional row does not account for the rank (\ExtRfourCond{}, within one \ExtRfourCondWithin{}), so the miss is not localised beyond the departure: N2 misses about as much with the first layer measured, frozen or decaying.
Post hoc, the tanh miss is about three times the truth's largest sample sensitivity (distance to the interval of the two truths \ExtRfourBracketTanh{}, base-to-floor spread at most \ExtRfourFloorTanhMax{}), so the ill-posed truth does not explain it.
The baselines from the initial statistics, $r_\text{init}$ (\ExtRfourRinit{}), the LN fixed-point set (\ExtRfourLnInit{}) and the static bound (\ExtRfourStatic{}), err less on all runs (differences to the criterion $\ExtRfourRinitCI$, $\ExtRfourLnInitCI$, $\ExtRfourStaticCI$) and are not separated from it on the sample-robust runs ($\ExtRfourRobRinitCI$, $\ExtRfourRobLnInitCI$, $\ExtRfourRobStaticCI$), so we do not claim N2 beats the static bound here; the count at $0.1T$ errs least (\ExtRfourEarly{}, within one \ExtRfourEarlyWithin{}) and also misses the tolerance.
The criterion beats the \ExtRfourMuCount{} plain $\mu$ variants (\ExtRfourMuMin{}--\ExtRfourMuMax{}) and is not separated from the signed and exact forms with the gain inserted, our adaptation (\ExtRfourMuLn{}, $\ExtRfourMuLnCI$), so rung 4 supports no claim for or against $\mu$.
Observed per cell, not registered: on ReLU, where the truth is nearly well-posed, the criterion errs by \ExtRfourReluDecay{} (within one \ExtRfourReluDecayWithin{}), the static bound (\ExtRfourFrStatic{}, error/within one) and the LN set read at $T$ (\ExtRfourFrLnT{}) come within the tolerance on field ReLU, and no row does on latent ReLU (best \ExtRfourLrLnInit{}).
The limits: the tanh rank is not settled at rung 2's horizon and is sample-sensitive, no longer-horizon or interval-scored test was run, and which of the reduced flow's two approximations (the Gaussian-target gain and the diagonal coupling) breaks is untested.
No further rung 4 work is planned.

\paragraph{E8 and the ViT race (development).}
\Cref{fig:ext-e8} shows the per-epoch count of every E8 row (the constant schedules end at $\ExtEeightTlo$ vs $\ExtEeightThi$ directions in T, $\ExtEeightPlo$ vs $\ExtEeightPhi$ in P); after a decrease T's lost directions keep shrinking at $\ExtEeightTdecay$ decades per unit of learning-rate time, and P's would need \ExtEeightPneedTen{} ($e=10$) and $\ExtEeightPneedTwenty$ ($e=20$) times the learning-rate time left to close the gap.
In development data that no test has scored, the per-direction drive and self-prediction parts of the linear model have reversed signs in most counted ViT directions during the first epochs, and early-training force readouts do not beat E3's step-0 rule; a race read instead from each direction's log headroom against the decoupled decay budget $2\sum_t\mathrm{lr}_t\,\mathrm{wd}$, with no fitted constant, places Arm T's weight-decay transition at \ExtSevenHeadLo--\ExtSevenHeadHi{} against \ExtSevenMeas{} measured (step-0 rule: \ExtSevenStep) and fails in Arm P.
It has since been registered as E7 and scored once (below and \cref{sec:e8}).
In E8-R, a ramp of weight decay in Arm P, the count moves by $\ExtEeightRgap$ ($\ExtEeightRgapCI$), within the width ceiling, which is not informative.

\paragraph{The race in the ViT (E7).}
E7 registered the headroom race before any production run: direction $k$ survives to step $T$ if its log headroom at $t_r=200$, $\log(e_k(t_r)/e_{\max})$, stays above $\log10^{-3}$ after the decoupled decay budget $2\sum_{t_r\le t<T}\mathrm{lr}_t\,\mathrm{wd}$; $t_r$ and the transition definition were chosen while looking at E3.
The grid has 40 Arm T runs at eight $\sqrt2$-spaced decays $0.0012$--$0.0136$ (5 seeds, read at epochs 20 and 50) and 15 toggle runs from Arm T towards Arm P at $\mathrm{wd}=0.0034$ (3 seeds).
Five amendments precede any production value; the last fixed a crash of the single scoring pass, which showed only that all runs were complete and that H1 on the horizon takes its pass branch, so that verdict was known before the rescore and the others are prospective.
H1: $\lambda_c$, the decay at which the seed-mean count falls below half the initial one, is \ExtSevLcTw{} ($\ExtSevLcTwCI$) at epoch 20, inside the band around \ExtSevPredTw{} and a fresh-seed replication of E3's \ExtSevenMeas{} rather than a prospective test, and \ExtSevLcFi{} ($\ExtSevLcFiCI$) at epoch 50, inside the band $[0.00135,0.0054]$ around the prediction \ExtSevPredFi{}.
The ratio \ExtSevRatio{} ($\ExtSevRatioCI$) excludes every horizon-independent criterion (static bound, capacity, the initial-time $\mu$ counts, which predict 1; no $\mu$ variant and no count at $t_r$ crosses inside the grid, and the step-0 rule gives no ratio in \ExtSevStepCens{} of seed draws), though not an account in which the early spectrum keeps decaying.
Only a ratio below one was registered; the race's own ratio, \ExtSevRaceRatio{} ($\ExtSevRaceRatioCI$), lies above the measured one by \ExtSevGap{} (post hoc; $\ExtSevGapCI$ from a joint seed resample, $\ExtSevGapCIRow$ resampling runs within rows), so the transition moves further with training length than the decay budget accounts for, by about a fifth of the log shift (the race gives \ExtSevLogShare{}).
H2 (per-run count error; intervals are seed-cluster bootstrap with $t$ intervals of 4 df beside them, and a verdict needs both): the race errs by \ExtSevMaeTw{} and \ExtSevMaeFi{} at epochs 20 and 50 and beats the count read at $t_r$ (\ExtSevEarlyTw{} and \ExtSevEarlyFi{}, the comparison registered as decisive), our index pairings of the $\mu$ criterion (\ExtSevMuDiagTw{}, \ExtSevMuDiagFi{} and \ExtSevMuWdTw{}, \ExtSevMuWdFi{}), our count on the signed drive of \citet[App.~K.2]{demelocosta2026drive} (\ExtSevMuKtTw{}, \ExtSevMuKtFi{}) and the registered log-eigenvalue trend null (\ExtSevTrendTw{}, \ExtSevTrendFi{}, far off the scale, so weak evidence) as well as its closer log-time form (\ExtSevTrendLogTw{}, \ExtSevTrendLogFi{}).
Against their tight count with the run's decay floor, the registered $\mu$ baseline, it wins at epoch 20 (\ExtSevMuTrTw{}) and is undetermined at epoch 50 (\ExtSevMuTrFi{}; difference \ExtSevMuTrFiDiff{}, bootstrap $\ExtSevMuTrFiCI$, $t$ $\ExtSevMuTrFiCIt$); against E3's step-0 rule (\ExtSevStepTw{}, \ExtSevStepFi{}) it is undetermined at both horizons, i.e.\ not shown to beat it per run at 5 seeds.
These $\mu$ rows are counts we build from the linear criterion of \citet{demelocosta2026drive} and transfer to the ViT, where that work makes no rank prediction, so a win over them is not a test of its theory.
H3: the race's own $\lambda_c$ is \ExtSevHthTw{} ($\ExtSevHthTwCI$) and \ExtSevHthFi{} ($\ExtSevHthFiCI$) of the measured one, inside the factor 1.5 but early at both horizons, and the factor is only 6\% wider than the grid step; at the registered sensitivity thresholds the ratio is \ExtSevThrHiTw{} and \ExtSevThrHiFi{} at $10^{-4}$ but \ExtSevThrLoTw{} and \ExtSevThrLoFi{} at $10^{-2}$, outside it, while the horizon ratio stays below one at all three.
Post hoc, the error is one-sided: the race's seed-mean count is below the measured one in \ExtSevBelow{} row $\times$ horizon cells (signed error \ExtSevSigned{}), most at the lowest decays, where the measured count grows after $t_r$ (\ExtSevLowMeas{} at $\mathrm{wd}=0.0012$, epoch 20, against \ExtSevLowEarly{} at $t_r$), which a removal budget starting at $t_r$ cannot produce; there the count at $t_r$ is closer, and the win over it comes from the high-decay rows.
Toggles (exploratory, race error minus the reference row's, 2 sd $=\ExtSevTwoSd$): an EMA target and a target LayerNorm show no break at the registered threshold (\ExtSevEma{}, \ExtSevTln{}; post hoc, these lie between the 2 sd of the three seeds the toggles share, \ExtSevGThreeSd{}, and that of the reference row's five seeds, so the reading rests on the five-seed sd), predictor LayerNorms fall just outside (\ExtSevPln{}), removing decay from norm gains and biases breaks the race as registered (\ExtSevNodn{}; measured \ExtSevNodnMeas{}), and under AdamW the race equals the count at $t_r$ while the measured count is \ExtSevAdamMeas{}; each shows a change sufficient to break the readout (the mechanisms we name for them are post hoc), and Arm P's failure is not located to either.
The rows ran on mixed GPU families (P100, 1080, 2080 Ti, V100) with one software environment, the crossing rows of each $\lambda_c$ on one family or nearly so, and the toggles on a different family from their reference row; a rerun of the reference row on V100 (E7-G, registered before it ran) gives the P100 counts on each of its 3 seeds (differences \ExtSevGTw{} at epoch 20, \ExtSevGFi{} at epoch 50): no GPU-family difference beyond 2 sd was detectable at 3 seeds on that row (hardware plus packing and run-to-run nondeterminism; the training losses are not identical between the families, post hoc), so the small toggles' common shift in the measured count belongs to the toggles, not to the hardware, and their readings are unchanged against the V100 reference; the other rows' families stay confounded with decay.

